## 20. Scalability Plan

| Scale | Users | Architecture | Changes Required |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Prototype** | 10 | Single server, MongoDB Atlas free tier | Current architecture ✅ |
| **MVP** | 100 | Single server, Atlas M10 shared | Current architecture ✅ |
| **Growth** | 10,000 | Single server + Redis cache, Atlas M30 dedicated | Add Redis for event list caching, connection pooling, response compression |
| **Scale** | 100,000 | Horizontal: 3+ Node.js instances behind load balancer, Socket.IO Redis adapter, Atlas M50+ replica set | **Socket.IO Redis adapter** for cross-instance room broadcasting. Sticky sessions or Redis session store. CDN for static assets. |
| **Enterprise** | 1,000,000 | Kubernetes cluster, microservice split (Auth, Events, Bookings), MongoDB sharding, dedicated Socket.IO cluster, CDN, queue-based booking | **Major refactor**: Split monolith into microservices. Event-driven architecture with message queue (RabbitMQ/Kafka) for booking. Read replicas for event browsing. Sharding by event category or date range. |

### Critical Bottleneck at Scale

The `availableSeats` field on the Event document is a **write-hot field** — every booking writes to the same document. At very high concurrency (thousands of simultaneous bookings for one event), this becomes a serialization bottleneck at the MongoDB document lock level. 

**Mitigation at scale**: Implement a distributed counter pattern (e.g., sub-documents or a separate `SeatInventory` collection with sharding by event+partition).

